{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.69,
            0.046,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Square and A Cube"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.085,
            0.585,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": "Write the locker numbers that remain open."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.128,
            0.83,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "Khoisnam immediately collects word clues from these 10 lockers and reads, “The passcode consists of the first five locker numbers that were touched exactly twice.”"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.195,
            0.455,
            0.217
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Which are these five lockers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.22,
            0.829,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": "The lockers that are toggled twice are the prime numbers, since each prime number has 1 and the number itself as factors. So, the code is 2-3-5-7-11."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.302,
            0.435,
            0.327
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1.1 Square Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.335,
            0.849,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "Why are the numbers, 1, 4, 9, 16, …, called squares? We know that the number of unit squares in a square (the area of a square) is the product of its sides. The table below gives the areas of squares with different sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.421,
            0.846,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": "| Sidelength | Area |\n| --- | --- |\n| (in units) | (in sq units) |\n| 1 | 1 × 1 = 1 sq. unit |\n| 2 | 2 × 2 = 4 sq. units |\n| 3 | 3 × 3 = 9 sq. units |\n| 4 | 4 × 4 = 16 sq. units |\n| 5 | 5 × 5 = 25 sq. units |\n| 10 | 10 × 10 = 100 sq. units |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_table_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.684,
            0.567,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": "We use the following notation for squares."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.434,
            0.704,
            0.56,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "1 \\times 1 = 1 ^ { 2 } = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.434,
            0.722,
            0.562,
            0.74
          ],
          "content": "2 \\times 2 = 2^{2} = 4",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.434,
            0.74,
            0.565,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": "3 \\times 3 = 3 ^ { 2 } = 9 ,",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.758,
            0.573,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": "4 \\times 4 = 4 ^ { 2 } = 1 6",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.776,
            0.576,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "5 \\times 5 = 5^{2} = 25.",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.817,
            0.846,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": "In general, for any number n, we write $n \\times n = n ^ { 2 } ,$ , which is read as ‛n squared’."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.838,
            0.639,
            0.86
          ],
          "content": "Can we have a square of sidelength $\\frac{3}{5}$ or 2.5 units?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.861,
            0.689,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "Yes, there area in square units are $( \\frac { 3 } { 5 } ) ^ { 2 } = ( \\frac { 3 } { 5 } ) \\times ( \\frac { 3 } { 5 } ) = ( \\frac { 9 } { 2 5 } ) ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.89,
            0.443,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "and $(2.5)^{2} = (2.5) \\times (2.5) = 6.25.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.884,
            0.915,
            0.906,
            0.937
          ],
          "content": "3"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
